# 98. Endpoint corridors are bridge-rich or rhombus-rich

## 98.1 Route-level endpoint stability

At the sharp Section-96 wrapping endpoint put

\[
\theta_4=2\arcsin\mu_4,
\qquad
s_4:=\sin\theta_4=0.658020712164235\ldots,
\]

and let `W_{1,D},W_{2,D},W_{3,D}` be the three distinguished outer-contact routes.
Canonical Section 96 and the exact local identity

\[
L_D+Q_D=4a_D-6-2s_D-2c_D
\]

give

\[
{a_D\over D}\to\alpha_4={3\over2}s_4,
\qquad
Q_D,s_D,c_D=o(D),
\qquad
L_D=6s_4D+o(D).
\tag{98.1}
\]

Each distinguished endpoint chord has length at least `s_4D-o(D)`.  Every remaining
attachment interval costs at least two outer-walk edges except for
`c_D^partial=o(D)` direct loaded-loaded sides.  Therefore the three distinguished
route lengths `ell_{j,D}` satisfy

\[
\sum_{j=1}^3\ell_{j,D}\le3s_4D+o(D).
\]

Since each route dominates its endpoint chord, separately

\[
\boxed{
\ell_{j,D}=s_4D+o(D),
\quad
d_{j,D}=s_4D+o(D),
\quad
e_{j,D}:=\ell_{j,D}-d_{j,D}=o(D).
}
\tag{98.2}

For one route orient its unit edge vectors `z_k` along the chord direction `q`, and
write `psi_k` for the signed angle from `q` to `z_k`.  Projection gives

\[
\sum_k(1-\cos\psi_k)=e_{j,D}.
\tag{98.3}
\]

Since `1-cos x>=2x^2/pi^2` on `[-pi,pi]`,

\[
\boxed{
\sum_k\psi_k^2=o(D).
}
\tag{98.4}
\]

The path lies in the ellipse with its endpoints as foci and major-axis length
`ell_{j,D}`, so its transverse distance from the chord is

\[
O\!\left(\sqrt{d_{j,D}e_{j,D}+e_{j,D}^2}\right)=o(D).
\tag{98.5}
\]

Thus every special route is a thin macroscopic contact corridor, and for every fixed
angular tolerance only `o(D)` route-edge occurrences deviate from its chord direction
by that tolerance.

## 98.2 Nonbridge corridor edges almost always border strict rhombi

Let `H_D` be the induced contact graph on the selected unloaded harmonic component and
its attachment set.  A nonbridge outer-face edge has one bounded face on its inner
side.  Canonical Section 82 says every bounded four-face is a strict unit rhombus.
If `f_exc,D` is the number of nonquadrilateral bounded faces, then

\[
f_{exc,D}\le Q_D,
\qquad
\sum_{f\ nonquad}\deg f=4f_{exc,D}+Q_D\le5Q_D=o(D).
\tag{98.6}
\]

Hence only `o(D)` nonbridge outer-edge occurrences can border a nonquadrilateral
bounded face.

Fix one distinguished route and let `B_D` be its number of bridge occurrences.  For
any fixed `eta in (0,s_4)`, along a subsequence one has either

\[
\boxed{B_D\ge\eta D}
\tag{98.7}
\]

or else at least

\[
\boxed{(s_4-\eta-o(1))D}
\tag{98.8}

route-edge occurrences border strict rhombi.  Intersecting with (98.4), for every
fixed `delta>0` the latter branch retains the same leading count with exposed unit-side
direction within `delta` of the corridor chord.  Since one rhombus has four sides,
there are at least

\[
\left({s_4-\eta\over4}-o(1)\right)D
\tag{98.9}
\]

distinct boundary strict rhombi of that phase.

## 98.3 A bridge-rich corridor has linear block-tree depth

A bridge cannot cut off an attachment-free component of `H_D`.  If it did, every
vertex on that side would be unloaded and positive harmonic equilibrium would give a
connected network with only one boundary value across the bridge.  The discrete
maximum principle would collapse all those vertices to that boundary point,
contradicting source distinctness.

Bridge cuts also cannot alternate their two attachment sets around `partial P_D`:
connected paths joining alternating boundary pairs in the two bridge-deletion
components would have to cross in the plane.

Contract maximal bridge-free blocks of `H_D`; the bridges form a tree `T_D`.  Project
a distinguished outer route to this tree.  If a bridge is traversed out and back,
its two route edge vectors are opposites.  Their contribution to (98.3) is exactly

\[
(1-\cos\psi)+(1+\cos\psi)=2.
\]

Because the total route excess is `o(D)`, only `o(D)` bridge edges can be traversed
both ways.  Therefore in the bridge-rich case (98.7), after deleting `o(D)`
backtracking occurrences at least

\[
\boxed{(\eta-o(1))D}
\tag{98.10}

distinct bridge edges lie on the simple `T_D` path between the endpoint blocks.  The
corresponding bridge cuts form a nested linear chain of nontrivial, nonalternating
attachment separators.

## 98.4 Exact surviving split

Every distinguished Section-96 corridor therefore has one of two concrete forms:

1. **bridge-rich:** a linear-depth laminar attachment-cut chain along an almost-straight
   contact corridor; or
2. **rhombus-rich:** a linear family of boundary strict rhombi with exposed sides in an
   arbitrarily narrow projective direction cone around the corridor chord.

The theorem does not assume 2-connectivity, discard bridges, or use the false global
claim `L_D-per(P_D)=o(D)`.  Contact-isolated source points are outside the harmonic
cell and play no role.

